      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, let's tackle this with a little bit of a \"goblin touch.\" Imagine your stock levels as a treasure hoard guarded by a mischievous goblin. To keep the goblin happy and prevent it from stealing all your treasures, you'll need to calculate the right amount of safety stock. \n\nFirst, let's consider the average demand during the lead time. This is like figuring out how many goblins are likely to raid your hoard in a given period. Then, we need to account for the variability in demand and lead time, which is like the goblins' unpredictable behavior. We can use a formula like this:\n\nSafety Stock = Z * σLT * √(LT)\n\nWhere:\n- Z is the desired service level (the confidence level you want to maintain, like how confident you want to be that the goblins won't raid your hoard)\n- σLT is the standard deviation of demand during the lead time (how much the goblins' raiding behavior varies)\n- LT is the lead time (how long it takes for the goblins to get your shipment)\n\nBy plugging in your numbers, you'll get a good estimate of how much safety stock you need to keep the goblins at bay. Remember, the higher",
